// Festivals (#3000): the real-world festivals the game marks with a mood, a few extra things in the world and a short,
// kid-friendly explanation (festival.<key>.name / .explain / .vega in data/locales). The calendar sets the DEFAULT only —
// a world admin can switch any festival on by hand (/festival <key>), so nothing is ever missed (no FOMO).
//
// Windows are whole UTC days. "fixed": month/day every year (+ daysBefore/daysAfter, or until a fixed month/day);
// "weekday": the first <weekday> on or after month/day; "dates": explicit yyyy-MM-dd ranges for the lunar and other movable
// calendars — covered for datesFrom..datesThrough, extended every year (FestivalContentTests reminds us).
//
// "My festivals" (#3021): "big" marks the fourteen big world festivals and "locales" the language communities that mainly
// celebrate a festival ("*" = everybody) — together the default selection of every player. "priority" (#3022) lets Halloween
// and Christmas lead the chip and the mood over a festival that starts inside their week; "coreDays"/"coreWeekday" in the
// effects keep the mood to a festival's first days (default 3, 0 = none) or its Sundays (Advent). "pack" (#3023) names a
// challenge pack of the festival's own (festival_<key>), which then no longer starts its theme's pack.
//
// Movable dates (checked 2026-10-29): Lunar New Year 2027-02-06 / 2028-01-26 and the Lantern Festival 2027-02-20 /
// 2028-02-09 (chinatravel.com, publicholidays.com.my); Holi 2027-03-22/23, 2028-03-11 (publicholidays.in, calendardate.com);
// Diwali 2026-11-08, 2027-10-29, 2028-10-17 (publicholidays.com); Hanukkah 2026-12-04…12, 2027-12-24…2028-01-01,
// 2028-12-12…20 (hebcal.com); Ramadan 2027-02-08…03-08 with Eid al-Fitr 2027-03-09/10, Ramadan 2028-01-28…02-26 with Eid
// 2028-02-27 (thenationalnews.com, awarenessdays.com — astronomical estimates, the real day follows the moon sighting);
// Chuseok / Mid-Autumn 2026-09-25, 2027-09-15, 2028-10-03 (publicholidays.co.kr); Qixi / Chilseok 2026-08-19, 2027-08-08,
// 2028-08-26 (en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Qixi_Festival); Vesak 2027-05-20, 2028-05-23 (publicholidays.lk, full-moon reckoning);
// Dragon Boat 2027-06-09, 2028-05-28 (travelchinaguide.com); Sukkot 2027-10-15…22, 2028-10-04…11 (hebcal.com); Loy Krathong
// 2026-11-25, 2027-11-14, 2028-11-02 (umetravel.com); Easter 2027-03-28, 2028-04-16, Orthodox Easter 2027-05-02, 2028-04-16
// (publicholidays.com); Carnival Monday 2027-02-08, 2028-02-28 and Maslenitsa 2027-03-08…14, 2028-02-21…27 derived from
